  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 251 PM MDT Sat Aug 22 2026\n\nSynoptically, the ridge to the west is forecast to mainly remain \nstationary over the Four Corners region, keeping us in \nnorthwesterly flow throughout the period. Guidance indicates the \nridge may begin to amplify on Wednesday, and begin to de-amplify \ncome Friday as a trough moves through the US/Canadian Border. \nShortwaves are expected to move through the area during the week and \ninto the weekend, increasing precipitation chances. \n\nHigh temperatures for the period will generally remain in the 90s. \nWednesday appears to be the \"coolest\" day, with highs ranging from \nthe mid 80s to low 90s. Friday is forecast to be the warmest, with \nhighs in the upper 90s and the potential for a few locations to \nreach the low 100s. If the ridge begins to move eastward, then \nhigher than forecasted temperatures could be seen. Winds are \nexpected to remain calm to breezy throughout the period. \n\nPrecipitation chances are present throughout the period. Wednesday \nand Thursday hold the highest potential, with PoPs in the 20-50% \nrange, as shortwaves move through the area during the afternoon and \nevening hours. While instability and shear parameters appear \nsufficient for severe storms, model guidance suggests some \nvariability in CAPE and shear values, which could lead to less \norganized storms and reduced severe chances. Precipitation chance \nthen begins to taper off to 5-15% for Friday and Saturday.",
